@* One title's whole relationship with Up Next, in one control.

   A toggle rather than a one-way add, because a backlog row is where the mistake
   is noticed: queueing something and immediately reconsidering used to mean
   leaving the page for Up Next, finding the row again there, and pressing a
   different button with a different glyph. The row already says "Queued", so the
   control beside it has to be able to undo that.

   Plus and minus rather than a cross, and the distinction is not cosmetic. The
   cross on Up Next removes a slot from an ordered list, which is that page's
   subject; here the pair reads as one state — in the queue, or not — which is the
   only thing a backlog row has to say about it. Neither touches the library.

   It exists as a component because the backlog row and the relatives inside an
   expansion need exactly the same three states, and two copies of "when may this
   be pressed" is how the badge and the button start disagreeing. *@

<button class="icon add @(IsQueued ? "dequeue" : null)"
        disabled="@(!Enabled)"
        title="@Label"
        aria-label="@Label"
        @onclick="ToggleAsync"><Icon Name="@(IsQueued ? "minus" : "plus")" /></button>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public int AnimeId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Named in the accessible label, because "add" alone says nothing on a page of fifty.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public LibraryStatus Status { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool IsQueued { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Called with the title and where it should end up.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<(int AnimeId, bool Queued)> OnToggle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether pressing it can do anything.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// It answers the same question <see cref="IQueueService.AddAnimeAsync"/>
    /// answers server-side, so an enabled press always works and a disabled one was
    /// never going to. Deciding it here rather than reporting the refusal afterwards
    /// is what lets the backlog have no message banner at all: the outcomes are
    /// "queued", "unqueued", and "the button was greyed out and said why".
    ///
    /// Leaving the queue is always allowed, whatever the status. A title that stopped
    /// being planned while it sat in a slot is exactly the row somebody would want to
    /// clear by hand rather than wait for the next sync to advance past.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool Enabled => IsQueued || Status == LibraryStatus.Planning;

    private string Label =>
        IsQueued ? $"Remove {Title} from Up Next"
        : Status != LibraryStatus.Planning
            ? $"{Title} is {Status.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()}, so it is not waiting to be watched"
            : $"Add {Title} to Up Next";

    private Task ToggleAsync() => OnToggle.InvokeAsync((AnimeId, !IsQueued));
}
